    <string name="stun_assessment_cancelled">Test cancelled</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_favorable">Endpoint-independent behaviour is friendly to calls, WebRTC, and peer-to-peer games. Direct UDP connections are more likely, reducing reliance on relays.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_moderate">This common filtering policy usually works with ICE, but peers must coordinate the connection correctly. Some sessions may fall back to a relay, adding latency.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_restrictive">The public mapping changes with the destination. Calls and peer-to-peer games may need TURN or another relay, which can add latency; applications without a relay may fail to connect.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_open">The socket appears directly reachable without address translation. Firewall filtering still affects which incoming UDP replies are accepted.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_inconsistent">The tested paths produced different complete classifications, so there is no single reliable assessment. This can happen with layered NAT, multiple routes, or changing network state; retry the test.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_basic">At least one server found a public UDP endpoint, but none could run all RFC 5780 probes. Calls or games may still work, but this test cannot predict whether they will connect directly or use a relay.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_failed">All selected servers failed or timed out. This can mean UDP is blocked, the network is unavailable, or the servers cannot be reached; it does not prove a specific NAT type.</string>
    <string name="stun_impact_cancelled">No new assessment was produced. Previous results, if shown, belong to the earlier completed run.</string>
